.TH iron-vault-import 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-import \- Make the vault from a migration bundle that `export` wrote on another machine. The service prompts for the export passphrase, shows a summary, prompts for a new vault passphrase, shows a new recovery key once on the controlling terminal and, once you confirm you saved it, writes the vault, the source\*(Aqs credentials and approval counters included. It needs a controlling terminal, and leaves the service Locked. The vault routes nothing until you approve a config with `config approve`, at a version above every one the source reserved. The BUNDLE must be a regular file of at most 260 MiB. Before it connects, the command creates `<BUNDLE>.config.toml` beside the bundle with mode 0600, and refuses a name already taken. After the import it fills that copy from the bundle\*(Aqs config, checked against the committed config\*(Aqs digest, and prints the `config approve` command for it; if the copy cannot be made it removes it and prints the digest to check your own copy of the source\*(Aqs config against, and still exits 0. Any other ending removes the copy. The first approval lists the whole config as added, since nothing is active
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault import\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-\-host\-unlock\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIBUNDLE\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Make the vault from a migration bundle that `export` wrote on another machine. The service prompts for the export passphrase, shows a summary, prompts for a new vault passphrase, shows a new recovery key once on the controlling terminal and, once you confirm you saved it, writes the vault, the source\*(Aqs credentials and approval counters included. It needs a controlling terminal, and leaves the service Locked. The vault routes nothing until you approve a config with `config approve`, at a version above every one the source reserved. The BUNDLE must be a regular file of at most 260 MiB. Before it connects, the command creates `<BUNDLE>.config.toml` beside the bundle with mode 0600, and refuses a name already taken. After the import it fills that copy from the bundle\*(Aqs config, checked against the committed config\*(Aqs digest, and prints the `config approve` command for it; if the copy cannot be made it removes it and prints the digest to check your own copy of the source\*(Aqs config against, and still exits 0. Any other ending removes the copy. The first approval lists the whole config as added, since nothing is active.
.PP
With \-\-host\-unlock, the vault also enrols the identity in the unlock handoff as a recipient, as `init \-\-host\-unlock` does, so a restart unlocks with no owner present. The service refuses to start the import if the handoff is absent or untrusted, before any prompt.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-\-host\-unlock\fR
Also enrol the identity in the unlock handoff as a recipient. A dev\-mode build does not accept this flag
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fIBUNDLE\fR>
The bundle file to read
